1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,060
Okay, the final section of this chapter deals with the liver.

2
00:00:03,380 --> 00:00:04,720
The first topic is jaundice.

3
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,900
Jaundice is yellow discoloration of the skin.

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:10,060
The earliest sign of jaundice is scleral icterus,

5
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:12,820
or a yellow discoloration of the sclera of the eyes.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:15,800
Jaundice is due to an increased serum bilirubin,

7
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:18,720
classically greater than 2.5 milligrams per deciliter.

8
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:21,200
And because it's due to an increase in serum bilirubin,

9
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:24,580
we're going to see it when there's disturbances in bilirubin metabolism,

10
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:27,220
as is going to become very clear as we move forward.

11
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:29,400
Here's a picture of a patient with jaundice.

12
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:30,520
Notice the yellow skin.

13
00:00:30,940 --> 00:00:33,300
In addition, notice that they have scleral icterus,

14
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:36,040
or a yellow discoloration of the sclera of the eyes.

15
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:39,080
Now, in order to understand the causes of jaundice,

16
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:41,300
you're going to need to understand bilirubin metabolism.

17
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:45,780
Let's remind ourselves that red blood cells live for approximately 120 days.

18
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:48,860
When it's time for the red blood cell to be removed from the blood,

19
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:53,500
it's taken away by the macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system.

20
00:00:53,960 --> 00:00:57,240
Of course, the most prominent location of these macrophages would be in the spleen.

21
00:00:57,240 --> 00:01:01,780
When the macrophages consume the red blood cell, they consume the hemoglobin as well.

22
00:01:02,260 --> 00:01:06,640
The hemoglobin is then broken down into heme plus globin.

23
00:01:06,980 --> 00:01:10,860
The globin is simply protein, and so it will be broken down into amino acids,

24
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:12,500
and the amino acids will be recycled.

25
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,760
The heme, of course, consists of iron and protoporphyrin.

26
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:22,600
The iron will be recycled, and the protoporphyrin will eventually be converted to unconjugated bilirubin.

27
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:28,780
This unconjugated bilirubin will be taken by albumin in the serum and delivered to the liver.

28
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:32,660
When it gets to the liver, the liver will conjugate the bilirubin

29
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:37,700
and eventually dump it into a space called the bile caniculi.

30
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:41,940
Now, from the bile caniculi, it will eventually go into the bile ducts

31
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:46,400
and eventually be stored in the gallbladder for eventual use during digestion.

32
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:52,060
Of course, at the appropriate time, bile will then be released into the small bowel to aid in digestion.

33
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:55,040
Now, when bile is released into the duodenum,

34
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:59,420
the intestinal flora take the conjugated bilirubin present within the bile,

35
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:01,500
and they convert it to urobilinogen.

36
00:02:01,940 --> 00:02:06,460
The urobilinogen then makes the stool brown and is partially resorbed into the blood

37
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:09,500
and then filtered by the kidney, making the urine yellow.

38
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:13,840
With this background in mind, we can now cover some of the causes of jaundice.

39
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:18,700
The first is extravascular hemolysis, or ineffective erythropoiesis.

40
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:23,520
In extravascular hemolysis, you get excessive destruction of the red blood cells

41
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:25,380
by the reticuloendothelial system.

42
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:29,920
And in ineffective erythropoiesis, you get death of red blood cells within the bone marrow

43
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:33,540
due to some ineffective ability to form the red blood cells, for example,

44
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:37,060
which then again will also result in consumption of the red blood cells

45
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:38,820
by the macrophages of the bone marrow.

46
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:42,420
In either case, the idea is that you're going to get excessive production

47
00:02:42,420 --> 00:02:43,920
of unconjugated bilirubin.

48
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,740
Now, these high levels of unconjugated bilirubin,

49
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:49,300
they're going to overwhelm the conjugating ability of the liver.

50
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:53,160
That's going to result in a high unconjugated bilirubin in the blood,

51
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:55,080
of course, giving the patient jaundice.

52
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:58,760
Just to illustrate this, let's pretend that the reticuloendothelial system

53
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:02,280
normally makes 50 molecules of unconjugated bilirubin per minute.

54
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:04,720
And let's pretend that the liver has the ability

55
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:07,100
to conjugate 75 molecules per minute.

56
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:10,720
So normally, you wouldn't see a buildup of unconjugated bilirubin in the blood.

57
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:16,120
However, if the patient is now producing 100 molecules of unconjugated bilirubin per minute,

58
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:18,880
that's going to overwhelm the liver's ability to conjugate.

59
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:23,420
And so therefore, you're going to see excessive unconjugated bilirubin within the blood.

60
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:26,240
And of course, that's what we see in this particular circumstance.

61
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:30,740
Now, this unconjugated bilirubin will eventually be conjugated by the liver

62
00:03:30,740 --> 00:03:32,340
because it has no place else to go.

63
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:35,740
Remember that unconjugated bilirubin is not water-soluble,

64
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:37,060
so it can't leak out in the urine.

65
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:41,480
It simply just floats around in the blood until the liver finally gets a chance to conjugate it.

66
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:44,780
As the liver conjugates all of this unconjugated bilirubin,

67
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:48,640
you're now going to get excessive conjugated bilirubin within the bile.

68
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:53,000
And so that's going to increase the concentration of conjugated bilirubin within the bile,

69
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:56,240
increasing the risk for pigmented gallstones.

70
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:02,180
Now, these patients will have very high concentrations of conjugated bilirubin within the bile.

71
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:07,300
When that is released into the duodenum, they're going to get excessive production of urobilinogen.

72
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:11,720
That excessive urobilinogen will also eventually be resorbed into the blood,

73
00:04:12,020 --> 00:04:14,760
filtered by the kidney, and then will make the urine dark.

74
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:16,920
So that's an important finding as well.

75
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:22,000
So two important consequences, dark urine and an increased risk for pigmented gallstones.

76
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:25,460
Now, board examiners like you to know that when this urine is dark,

77
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:31,520
it is due to the excessive urine urobilinogen and is not due to the unconjugated bilirubin.

78
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:35,360
Because remember that the unconjugated bilirubin is not water-soluble,

79
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:37,420
and therefore it can't enter into the urine.

80
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:39,520
Examiners will test you on that point.

81
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:43,540
The next disorder that results in jaundice is physiologic jaundice of the newborn.

82
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:49,280
Now, the idea here is that the newborn liver has a relatively low conjugating ability.

83
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:55,880
Recall that the enzyme that performs a conjugation is UGT, or uridine glucuronyl transferase.

84
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:59,520
Now, in the newborn liver, the activity of UGT is relatively low,

85
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:03,400
and so the patients can develop an increase in unconjugated bilirubin.

86
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:05,020
Now, let's just illustrate this.

87
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:10,240
We said that the normal, for example, would be 50 molecules of unconjugated bilirubin

88
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:12,900
being produced by the reticuloendothelial cells.

89
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:17,580
And let's pretend that the normal liver has the ability to conjugate 75 molecules per minute.

90
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:21,400
And so, again, there would be no backup of unconjugated bilirubin in the system.

91
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:26,980
However, if you have an immature liver and the liver is only able to conjugate, for example, 40 per minute,

92
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:30,400
now you're going to get backup of unconjugated bilirubin into the blood,

93
00:05:30,740 --> 00:05:35,080
which is then, of course, going to result in increased unconjugated bilirubin.

94
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:39,760
Now, in this particular case, the unconjugated bilirubin will have no place to go.

95
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:43,560
Again, it's fat-soluble, and it cannot leak out through the urine.

96
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:48,300
Now, in a newborn, the key complication will be that this unconjugated bilirubin,

97
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:51,300
because it's fat-soluble, can actually deposit within the brain,

98
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:53,420
particularly within the basal ganglia.

99
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:56,200
And that can lead to neurologic deficits in death,

100
00:05:56,460 --> 00:05:58,700
and this entire sequence is called crinicterous.

101
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:03,020
And so there's a concern for the development of crinicterous in a child

102
00:06:03,020 --> 00:06:05,580
that has an increase in unconjugated bilirubin.

103
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:07,940
The treatment, of course, is phototherapy.

104
00:06:08,260 --> 00:06:10,900
It's very high yield to note, for the purposes of board exams,

105
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:14,700
that the phototherapy does not conjugate the bilirubin.

106
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:18,500
It merely makes the unconjugated bilirubin water-soluble.

107
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:22,040
And so by altering the molecule so that it becomes water-soluble,

108
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:23,840
it can then leak out in the urine.

109
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:27,400
The next disorder that can give you jaundice is Gilbert syndrome.

110
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:30,920
Now, in Gilbert syndrome, the patients have a genetically low,

111
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,400
autosomal recessive, mild decrease in UGT conjugating activity.

112
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:37,420
Of course, if the liver can't conjugate,

113
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:39,960
there'll be an increase in unconjugated bilirubin.

114
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:43,400
Now, normally what happens, again, is that you've, let's say, for example,

115
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:46,760
produce 50 molecules of unconjugated bilirubin per minute.

116
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:48,540
Again, this is an arbitrary number.

117
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:51,700
And your liver normally has the ability to conjugate 75.

118
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:55,120
In these patients, they can only conjugate, for example, 50.

119
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:57,820
And so for the most part, they don't actually have jaundice.

120
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:01,280
However, were a stress to arise, for example, severe infection,

121
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:03,360
then the patients could develop jaundice.

122
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:08,620
Now, in Krigler-Najar syndrome, you have a more severe decrease in UGT.

123
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,640
In fact, there's an almost near absence of UGT.

124
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:13,780
And so if there's a near absence of UGT,

125
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:16,420
you're going to get very high levels of unconjugated bilirubin.

126
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:19,860
Of course, this is a genetic disorder, so that would be seen in the fetus.

127
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:21,980
And that could result in crinicterus,

128
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:24,900
because, of course, the unconjugated bilirubin is fat-soluble,

129
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,060
would deposit in the brain.

130
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:29,180
So Krigler-Najar syndrome is usually fatal.

131
00:07:29,740 --> 00:07:32,060
Now, I think of Gilbert syndrome and Krigler-Najar

132
00:07:32,060 --> 00:07:34,600
as sort of opposite extremes of a similar problem.

133
00:07:34,780 --> 00:07:37,380
Again, in Gilbert, you have mildly low UGT,

134
00:07:37,720 --> 00:07:39,600
whereas in Krigler-Najar, it's absent.

135
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:41,860
And now, a funny way that I bear this in mind

136
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:45,380
is sort of Krigler-Najar sounds like maybe like one of the villains

137
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:47,520
that you would hear about in a Disney movie or something.

138
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:52,020
And so I think of Krigler-Najar as sort of the evil version of this disease.

139
00:07:52,180 --> 00:07:55,420
And so, therefore, I remember it as a complete absence,

140
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:57,680
which would produce crinicterus, which would be fatal.

141
00:07:58,200 --> 00:07:59,840
So anyway, that's one way to think about it.

142
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:02,620
Dubin-Johnson syndrome can lead to jaundice.

143
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:06,800
And this is a deficiency of the bilirubin canicular transport protein.

144
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:08,600
It happens to be autosomal recessive.

145
00:08:08,820 --> 00:08:11,720
Let's go back and remind ourselves that the liver hepatocyte

146
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:15,720
takes unconjugated bilirubin and then eventually conjugates it.

147
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:17,720
Now, once it conjugates the bilirubin,

148
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:20,980
the next thing that it will do is to dump it into these bile caniculi,

149
00:08:21,340 --> 00:08:23,720
which eventually lead to the bile ducts.

150
00:08:23,980 --> 00:08:26,200
However, in order to put it in the bile caniculi,

151
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:28,140
you need the canicular transport protein.

152
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:30,940
So if this canicular transport protein is deficient,

153
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:34,060
conjugated bilirubin will build up within the liver cell

154
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:36,420
and will eventually leak out into the blood,

155
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:38,800
giving you a conjugated bilirubinemia.

156
00:08:39,420 --> 00:08:41,060
And so that's exactly what we say here.

157
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:43,840
You get an increase in conjugated bilirubin within the blood.

158
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:46,440
Now, this disorder is not clinically significant.

159
00:08:46,820 --> 00:08:49,260
However, there is one very high-yield point

160
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:51,940
concerning this disorder that shows up on the boards all the time,

161
00:08:52,300 --> 00:08:54,420
and that is that the liver will be pitch dark.

162
00:08:54,820 --> 00:08:55,860
So they'll tell you, for example,

163
00:08:55,940 --> 00:08:58,800
that a surgeon goes into the region for some other reason

164
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:02,700
and all of a sudden sees that there is a pitch black or pitch dark liver.

165
00:09:02,700 --> 00:09:05,760
And so, again, that would be suggestive of Dubin-Johnson syndrome.

166
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:08,540
Now, there's a derivative disorder called Rotor syndrome.

167
00:09:08,900 --> 00:09:10,820
It's the same thing as Dubin-Johnson.

168
00:09:11,100 --> 00:09:13,420
However, the liver lacks discoloration.

169
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:15,000
And so that's important to note

170
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:16,860
and can show up on examinations as well.

171
00:09:17,340 --> 00:09:19,800
Another cause of jaundice is biliary tract obstruction.

172
00:09:20,140 --> 00:09:22,580
The idea here is that you block the biliary tract,

173
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:23,380
and when you block it,

174
00:09:23,460 --> 00:09:25,720
whatever is in the bile will leak out into the blood.

175
00:09:26,020 --> 00:09:28,000
And, of course, one of the things present within bile

176
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,260
is conjugated bilirubin,

177
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:31,700
and so the conjugated bilirubin will go up.

178
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:34,360
Now, this is also known as obstructive jaundice,

179
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:36,280
and that's because you usually have an obstruction

180
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:37,500
that's blocking the tract.

181
00:09:37,860 --> 00:09:39,820
For example, patients can get gallstones

182
00:09:39,820 --> 00:09:42,680
or pancreatic carcinoma or cholangiocarcinoma,

183
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:46,000
which is a cancer of the biliary tract epithelium,

184
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:48,880
or parasites or the liver fluke, etc.

185
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:52,100
Each of these things can block the biliary tract,

186
00:09:52,440 --> 00:09:53,460
resulting in obstruction.

187
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:55,680
Now, when there's block of the biliary tract,

188
00:09:55,920 --> 00:09:57,560
a few important things are going to happen.

189
00:09:57,860 --> 00:09:59,500
First of all, whatever is in the bile

190
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:00,700
is going to leak into the blood.

191
00:10:00,700 --> 00:10:02,280
So, what are some things that are in bile?

192
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:04,180
Conjugated bilirubin is in bile,

193
00:10:04,300 --> 00:10:05,700
so that will go up within the blood.

194
00:10:06,180 --> 00:10:08,600
In addition, you have bile acids and bile salts

195
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:09,260
within the bile,

196
00:10:09,560 --> 00:10:11,460
so these will also leak into the blood,

197
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:13,900
and they will also be able to deposit

198
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:15,580
within the skin, creating pruritus.

199
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,240
Finally, there's a lot of cholesterol within the bile,

200
00:10:18,540 --> 00:10:20,160
so cholesterol will leak into the blood,

201
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:21,820
giving you hypercholesterolemia

202
00:10:21,820 --> 00:10:24,020
and xanthomas as well.

203
00:10:24,640 --> 00:10:27,040
Now, the second consequence of obstructive jaundice

204
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:29,820
will be that you won't be able to put bile into the bowel,

205
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:32,380
and so because you can't put bile into the bowel,

206
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:34,120
the patients are going to have pale stool,

207
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:36,640
and in addition, they're going to get steatorrhea

208
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:37,740
because you don't have bile,

209
00:10:37,820 --> 00:10:38,940
so you can't digest fat,

210
00:10:39,140 --> 00:10:41,940
and they'll get malabsorption of fat-soluble vitamins.

211
00:10:42,460 --> 00:10:43,520
Now, the final thing to note

212
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:45,200
is that the patients are going to have a dark urine.

213
00:10:45,540 --> 00:10:46,740
The reason they have a dark urine

214
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:48,700
is because conjugated bilirubin,

215
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:49,920
which has gone up in this case,

216
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:51,260
is water-soluble,

217
00:10:51,460 --> 00:10:52,740
so the urine will be dark.

218
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:55,200
However, again, the stool will be pale.

219
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:56,760
The final cause of jaundice

220
00:10:56,760 --> 00:10:58,040
that I've listed here in the table

221
00:10:58,040 --> 00:10:59,160
is viral hepatitis.

222
00:10:59,620 --> 00:11:01,580
Viral hepatitis causes inflammation

223
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:03,300
and disruption of the hepatocytes

224
00:11:03,300 --> 00:11:05,400
along with the small bile ductules,

225
00:11:05,900 --> 00:11:07,120
so therefore, what will happen

226
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:08,640
is that you're going to get an increase

227
00:11:08,640 --> 00:11:09,760
in both the conjugated

228
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:11,580
and the unconjugated bilirubin.

229
00:11:11,860 --> 00:11:12,960
Of course, the unconjugated

230
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:14,700
because you're damaging the hepatocytes,

231
00:11:15,160 --> 00:11:16,040
and the conjugated

232
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:16,900
because you're damaging

233
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:18,260
the small bile ductules.

234
00:11:18,820 --> 00:11:20,080
And now, in this particular case,

235
00:11:20,140 --> 00:11:21,020
the urine will be dark.

236
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:22,220
The reason why it's dark

237
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:23,040
is because you've got

238
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:24,220
conjugated bilirubin,

239
00:11:24,460 --> 00:11:25,680
and conjugated bilirubin

240
00:11:25,680 --> 00:11:26,660
is water-soluble,

241
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:28,120
so it will leak out in the urine.

242
00:11:28,860 --> 00:11:29,600
At the same time,

243
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:30,440
it's important to note

244
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:32,100
that the urine urobilinogen

245
00:11:32,100 --> 00:11:34,000
will actually be normal or decreased.

246
00:11:34,340 --> 00:11:35,040
Now, remember that

247
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:36,440
the urine urobilinogen

248
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:37,460
is a derivative

249
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:39,440
of how much conjugated bilirubin

250
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:40,520
you're actually putting

251
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:41,880
into the duodenum,

252
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:43,120
which would then get converted

253
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:44,800
to urine urobilinogen.

254
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:46,420
And now, in this particular case,

255
00:11:46,480 --> 00:11:47,820
you're not able to conjugate as much

256
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:49,300
because you damage the hepatocytes,

257
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:50,280
and at the same time,

258
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:51,600
the conjugated bilirubin

259
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:52,760
is leaking out into the blood,

260
00:11:53,140 --> 00:11:54,040
so less of it is able

261
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:55,160
to go into the duodenum,

262
00:11:55,480 --> 00:11:56,000
and so therefore,

263
00:11:56,180 --> 00:11:57,420
the urine urobilinogen

264
00:11:57,420 --> 00:11:59,160
will actually be normal

265
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:00,380
or slightly decreased.

